fof_version: '1.0'
type: enemy
id: veteran_soldier
name: Veteran Soldier
ruleset:
  modules:
  - id: base
    version: 1.0.0
enemy:
  # v0.3: Named, durability 4, attack +3, light armor, Telegraphed Finisher, rating 11.
  level: 4
  role: elite
  armor: 1
  morale: 9
  weapon: Soldier's sword
  wants: To be paid, to go home, and to finish this without a new scar.
  special: 'TELEGRAPHED FINISHER — once per scene, against a character already below half
    HP, it names the blow a full exchange early: the measured step, the blade drawn back.
    Next exchange it makes that one attack, and only that one, and a hit deals double damage.'
  when_bloodied: It stops committing and Defends (no attack; attacks on it are Hard) until
    someone in the party is below half HP; then it comes back for them.
  tells: It watches weight, not weapons, and it has not wasted a movement since the fight
    started.
  breaks: 'Withdraws in good order when the fight stops being worth the wound. It never routs:
    it walks backwards, blade up.'
  twists:
  - Three of them served together, and all three will leave at the same moment.
  - It recognises a party member's training, and says out loud where they learned it.
  - It is guarding someone it despises, for money it needs.
  - 'It carries an old wound: on a natural 12 against it, the wound opens and it withdraws.'
  - Its pension was stopped last month, and whoever stopped it is the party's real opponent.
  - It offers single combat, to spare the people it came with.
  nastier: Level 6, and it can name the Finisher twice a scene.
  description: >-
    A hardened fighter with years of real combat experience. Not a tactician — a survivor.
    Knows how to absorb punishment, how to read an opponent's weight shift, and exactly how
    long to wait before committing. Fights economically: no wasted motion, no bravado. Will
    press hard only when the opponent is already compromised. Difficult to rattle, difficult
    to put down.
  notes: >-
    Level 4 elite, armor 1: no overrides. One level above the latchman because it hit harder
    (attack +3) and carried a signature move.


    Trained, unhurried, and entirely without anything to prove. Not a tactician: a survivor.
    Its first target is the party member who looks most dangerous; it shifts to whoever is
    already hurt, and that is when the Finisher comes out, with the whole table given one
    exchange to answer it (Defend, Intercept, or get out of reach).


    A single elite is a real fight for a level 1 party of three; use it with company when
    you want a hard one.
  reskin_of: null
